# kills: the landscape deck strip scrolls rather than clips, at both ends
# suite: node --test --test-name-pattern ^the.landscape.deck.strip.scrolls.rather.than.clips..at.both.ends$ tests/e2e.test.js
# .decks goes overflow-x:auto -> hidden. The sibling mutant
# e_decks_strip_cannot_scroll.patch uses `clip` and records in its own header
# that `hidden` would have SURVIVED: an overflow:hidden box in Chrome is
# still programmatically scrollable, so nav.scrollLeft moves, maxScroll
# equals the overflow exactly, and both ends land flush. Every behavioural
# probe in the test reports a healthy scroller.
#
# What `hidden` actually removes is the user's half of it - no scrollbar, no
# drag, no wheel, no touch fling - so the 41px and 218px of chip that the
# narrow landscape rows push past the edge become unreachable by any gesture
# while remaining reachable from a script. That is why the test now asserts
# the computed overflow-x directly and not only its consequences, and this
# mutant is the proof that the direct assertion is the only thing catching it.
diff --git a/index.html b/index.html
--- a/index.html
+++ b/index.html
@@ -104,7 +104,7 @@ h1{font-family:Marcellus, serif; font-weight:400; font-size:clamp(20px,5.5vw,27p
 /* The chip row scrolls horizontally and never wraps; auto margins on the end
    chips keep it centred while it fits, without making either end unreachable
    once it overflows. The right edge fades so a cut-off chip reads as "more". */
-.decks{display:flex; gap:var(--sp-2); flex-wrap:nowrap; overflow-x:auto; scrollbar-width:none;
+.decks{display:flex; gap:var(--sp-2); flex-wrap:nowrap; overflow-x:hidden; scrollbar-width:none;
   -webkit-overflow-scrolling:touch; overscroll-behavior:contain;
   -webkit-mask-image:linear-gradient(90deg,#000 0 calc(100% - 22px), transparent 100%);
   mask-image:linear-gradient(90deg,#000 0 calc(100% - 22px), transparent 100%)}
